1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,476
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,476 --> 00:00:04,933
This is Devoker, a multiplayer Texas Hold'em game for playing with friends.

3
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:21,932
The game itself is small, but it's delivered the way a production service would be: containerised, deployed to Kubernetes with Helm, scanned for security issues in CI, rolled out by Argo CD through GitOps, monitored with Prometheus and Grafana, and backed by Terraform for the AWS side.

4
00:00:21,932 --> 00:00:29,422
In the next few minutes I'll walk through what it does, how it's built, and show every layer actually running on this machine.

5
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:31,737
Let's start with the product.

6
00:00:31,737 --> 00:00:36,361
A player enters a name and hosts a table, which gives them a five-letter room code.

7
00:00:36,361 --> 00:00:38,980
A friend enters the same code and asks to join.

8
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:42,991
The host sees the request appear instantly, and approves or declines it.

9
00:00:42,991 --> 00:00:45,776
Nobody sits at your table without your permission.

10
00:00:46,476 --> 00:00:49,623
Here's a hand in progress from two different browsers.

11
00:00:49,623 --> 00:00:52,654
On the left is Alice, the host; on the right is Bob.

12
00:00:52,654 --> 00:00:58,132
Blinds have been posted, the pot is thirty, and each player can see only their own hole cards.

13
00:00:58,132 --> 00:01:05,126
The server builds a separate view of the table for each player's secret token, so cards never leak to the wrong browser.

14
00:01:05,126 --> 00:01:08,856
Alice has the action, with fold, call, raise and all-in buttons.

15
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:13,095
After the flop, turn and river, the hand goes to showdown.

16
00:01:13,095 --> 00:01:20,781
The server evaluates both hands, Bob wins forty chips with one pair, and the result is recorded in PostgreSQL as hand history.

17
00:01:20,781 --> 00:01:25,540
Both screens update live through Server-Sent Events, without any page refresh.

18
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,738
Here's the architecture.

19
00:01:27,738 --> 00:01:30,111
A browser hits the Kubernetes Ingress.

20
00:01:30,111 --> 00:01:35,917
Slash goes to the React frontend served by nginx, which autoscales between two and five pods.

21
00:01:35,917 --> 00:01:41,973
Slash A P I goes to the FastAPI backend, running two replicas, and all state lives in PostgreSQL.

22
00:01:41,973 --> 00:01:55,895
Along the bottom is the delivery path: a git push triggers GitHub Actions, which tests, scans and builds the images, pushes them to the GitHub container registry, and then commits the new image tag back into the Helm chart.

23
00:01:55,895 --> 00:02:00,390
Argo CD, running inside the cluster, notices that commit and deploys it.

24
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:08,194
Prometheus scrapes the backend's metrics, Grafana visualises them, and Terraform describes the equivalent AWS infrastructure.

25
00:02:08,894 --> 00:02:11,943
The key backend decision is that the API is stateless.

26
00:02:11,943 --> 00:02:17,477
The first version kept games in memory, which meant only one replica and downtime on every deploy.

27
00:02:17,477 --> 00:02:22,559
Now every room is a single Postgres row containing the table as JSON and a version number.

28
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:32,045
Every change, like a bet or a fold, happens inside this locked helper, which takes a row lock with select for update, applies the change, bumps the version and commits.

29
00:02:32,045 --> 00:02:37,014
So two players acting at the same moment on different replicas simply queue on the lock.

30
00:02:37,014 --> 00:02:44,862
Each player's event stream watches the version column and pushes a fresh view whenever it changes, no matter which replica made the change.

31
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:46,295
Next, tests.

32
00:02:46,295 --> 00:02:56,430
The project shipped with thirty-four pytest tests covering the poker rules, hand rankings, and every API endpoint, and I added two more for a bug I'll show you later.

33
00:02:56,430 --> 00:03:05,527
The test fixture builds a throwaway SQLite database by running the real Alembic migrations, so the schema under test is exactly what production gets.

34
00:03:05,527 --> 00:03:08,824
Here they are, all thirty-six passing on this machine.

35
00:03:09,524 --> 00:03:12,552
For containers, both images use multi-stage builds.

36
00:03:12,552 --> 00:03:19,734
The backend installs its dependencies in a build stage and copies only the virtual environment into a slim runtime image.

37
00:03:19,734 --> 00:03:25,611
The frontend is compiled with Node, and only the static files ship, in an unprivileged nginx image.

38
00:03:25,611 --> 00:03:30,716
Both run as numeric, non-root users, which Kubernetes needs to verify run as non root.

39
00:03:30,716 --> 00:03:37,424
Docker Compose brings up Postgres, the backend and the frontend, and the backend waits until Postgres is healthy.

40
00:03:37,424 --> 00:03:42,707
The smoke script then plays a complete hand through the API, and here it reports success.

41
00:03:43,407 --> 00:03:44,333
Now Kubernetes.

42
00:03:44,333 --> 00:04:03,280
Everything is packaged as one Helm chart: deployments for the backend, frontend and Postgres, cluster IP services, an Ingress, a horizontal pod autoscaler, persistent volumes, a config map for blinds and starting chips, and a secret for the database password that's created with kubectl and never committed.

43
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:09,884
Here are the running pods: two backend replicas, two frontend pods managed by the autoscaler, and Postgres.

44
00:04:09,884 --> 00:04:16,056
Every container drops all Linux capabilities and runs as non-root with a read-only root file system.

45
00:04:16,056 --> 00:04:21,610
An init container waits for Postgres, so the backend never crash-loops on a fresh install.

46
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:26,878
And here's the same game served through the ingress controller at devoker dot localhost.

47
00:04:26,878 --> 00:04:35,391
The Ingress routes slash A P I to the backend and everything else to the frontend, with buffering turned off and a long read timeout so the event streams stay open.

48
00:04:36,091 --> 00:04:38,423
Delivery is handled by GitHub Actions.

49
00:04:38,423 --> 00:04:56,403
On every push, the pipeline runs the tests and a production frontend build, then four kinds of security scanning: bandit for static analysis, pip audit, npm audit and Trivy for vulnerable dependencies and Kubernetes misconfigurations, and Gitleaks to catch secrets anywhere in the git history.

50
00:04:56,403 --> 00:05:02,785
Only when all of that passes are the images built, for both amd64 and arm64, and scanned again by Trivy.

51
00:05:02,785 --> 00:05:06,529
Any fixable high or critical vulnerability stops the release.

52
00:05:06,529 --> 00:05:15,304
Images that pass are pushed to the GitHub container registry, tagged with the commit SHA, and a bot commits that tag into the Helm values file.

53
00:05:16,004 --> 00:05:22,768
Here's a real run on my fork of the repository, for the commit that pointed the pipeline at my own registry.

54
00:05:22,768 --> 00:05:32,664
Tests, static analysis, dependency scanning and the secrets scan all pass, both images build and clear the Trivy gate, and the deploy job commits the new tag.

55
00:05:32,664 --> 00:05:40,868
On the right is the published backend image on the GitHub container registry, tagged with the full commit SHA, for amd64 and arm64.

56
00:05:41,568 --> 00:05:43,833
That commit is where GitOps takes over.

57
00:05:43,833 --> 00:05:48,305
Argo CD runs inside the cluster and watches the Helm chart in the repository.

58
00:05:48,305 --> 00:05:52,950
When the image tag changes, it renders the chart and syncs the cluster to match.

59
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:59,454
Self-heal is on, so a manual kubectl edit gets reverted, and prune removes anything that git no longer contains.

60
00:05:59,454 --> 00:06:09,326
Here it's tracking my repository, synced and healthy at the bot's deploy commit, running the images the pipeline just built, including the bug fix you'll see in a moment.

61
00:06:10,026 --> 00:06:18,960
For observability, the backend exposes Prometheus metrics: request rates and latencies, plus custom metrics for hands played and open event streams.

62
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:27,472
The kube Prometheus stack is installed with Helm, and a ServiceMonitor in the Devoker chart tells Prometheus to scrape both backend replicas.

63
00:06:27,472 --> 00:06:36,406
The chart also ships a Grafana dashboard, and two alert rules: one for backend restarts, and one for the frontend autoscaler sitting at its maximum.

64
00:06:37,106 --> 00:06:38,890
Monitoring paid off right away.

65
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:45,797
When I pushed a burst of twenty-five games through the Ingress, about a third of the requests failed with five oh three.

66
00:06:45,797 --> 00:06:51,610
On the dashboard you can see why: p95 latency jumps to one second and fifteen event streams are open.

67
00:06:51,610 --> 00:06:53,912
The cause was the event stream endpoint.

68
00:06:53,912 --> 00:07:01,222
It took its database session through FastAPI's dependency injection, and FastAPI keeps that session open until the stream ends.

69
00:07:01,222 --> 00:07:05,366
So every connected player held a pooled connection, idle in transaction.

70
00:07:05,366 --> 00:07:17,280
Fifteen streams used up the whole pool, the readiness check had to wait for a connection, its one second probe timed out, Kubernetes marked both backends unready, and the Ingress had nowhere to send traffic.

71
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:24,647
The fix is small: the stream checks the player's token with its own short-lived session, just like the polling loop already did.

72
00:07:24,647 --> 00:07:33,108
After the fix, the same burst leaves no connections stuck, readiness answers in ten milliseconds, and twenty-five out of twenty-five games succeed.

73
00:07:33,108 --> 00:07:36,158
I added a regression test that fails on the old code.

74
00:07:36,858 --> 00:07:44,080
The fix then went out the proper way: pushed to git, through the full pipeline, into the registry, and deployed by Argo CD.

75
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:52,477
On the dashboard, the burst at twenty-three twenty-seven ran on the old image: latency stuck at a second and fifteen streams that never let go.

76
00:07:52,477 --> 00:08:01,637
The burst at twenty-three fifty ran on the fixed image from the pipeline: ten hands a minute, flat latency, and streams that close as soon as players leave.

77
00:08:01,637 --> 00:08:06,511
Twenty-five out of twenty-five games succeeded, with zero readiness probe failures.

78
00:08:07,211 --> 00:08:19,332
Finally, Terraform describes the cloud side: a VPC with two public subnets, an EKS cluster with a small managed node group, E C R repositories with scan on push, and an encrypted S3 bucket.

79
00:08:19,332 --> 00:08:22,731
The configuration formats cleanly and validates here.

80
00:08:22,731 --> 00:08:32,928
Applying it creates real, billable AWS resources, about fifteen cents an hour, so it's only planned and applied when needed, and destroyed in the same session.

81
00:08:33,628 --> 00:08:34,282
To wrap up.

82
00:08:34,282 --> 00:08:45,276
The biggest lesson is that the application's state model decides how it can be deployed: moving rooms into Postgres is what made two replicas and zero-downtime rolling updates possible.

83
00:08:45,276 --> 00:08:53,417
The security scans found real problems, and load testing against the dashboards found one more, which is now fixed and covered by a test.

84
00:08:53,417 --> 00:09:03,995
Everything you have seen here really ran: the tests, a green pipeline on GitHub, Docker Compose, the Helm chart on minikube, Argo CD deploying from git, and the monitoring stack.

85
00:09:03,995 --> 00:09:05,184
Thanks for watching.
